window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-8759974425fadfd1"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"learn-a-new-concept\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">学习一个新概念</h1> <p>将密集的资料整理为清晰、便于审阅的学习报告。</p> <p>使用 ChatGPT 学习研究论文或课程等资料，将阅读任务分配给子代理、补充背景，并生成包含图表的 Markdown 报告。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>想学习陌生概念的个人。</li> <li>需要并行阅读、补充背景、制作图表和撰写综合报告的密集资料。</li> <li>希望将一次性阅读整理成带引用和术语表、可反复使用的 Markdown 报告的人。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"skills-plugins\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">技能与插件</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>技能或插件</th> <th>用途</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>$imagegen</code></td> <td>如果 Mermaid 图表无法满足需要，可生成非精确的说明性视觉素材。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">起步提示词</h2> <p>分析研究论文并教我理解其中的概念</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">我想学习这篇研究论文中的一个新概念：[paper path or URL]（论文路径或 URL）。\n\n请按子代理工作流程执行：\n- 指定一个子代理梳理论文的问题陈述、贡献、方法、实验和局限。\n- 指定一个子代理整理我需要了解的前置背景和术语。\n- 指定一个子代理检查图、表、符号，以及需要谨慎核实的主张。\n- 等待所有子代理完成，协调彼此的不同意见，并避免作出超出来源材料的断言。\n\n最终输出：\n- 创建 `notes/[concept-name]-report.md`\n- 报告应包括执行摘要、术语表、论文逐步讲解、概念图、方法图、证据表、注意事项和待解问题\n- 适合用图时，使用 Markdown 原生 Mermaid 图表\n- 如果 Markdown 原生图表不足以表达内容，可使用 imagegen 生成说明性而非精确还原的视觉素材\n- 尽可能引用论文的章节、页码、图或表\n\n约束：\n- 如果证据薄弱，不要把论文中的说法当作事实\n- 将论文主张与 ChatGPT 的解释分开\n- 指出缺失的背景知识、假设和后续阅读建议\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">子代理</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"introduction\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">简介</h2> <p>学习一篇内容密集的论文或课程，不能只靠摘要。真正的目标是建立可以运用的思维模型：资料试图解决什么问题、方法实际如何运行、哪些证据支持相关主张、这些主张依赖哪些假设，以及哪些部分还需要继续调查。</p> <p>ChatGPT 可以自动收集背景资料，并把复杂概念转化为容易理解的图表或插图，因此很适合用来完成这项工作。这个用例也很适合使用<a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/agent-configuration/subagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">子代理（本地尚未收录）</a>：一个对话线程梳理论文结构，另一个整理前置知识，第三个检查图表和符号；主线程则协调结果，并生成供你之后审阅的报告。</p> <p>最终产物应便于以后重新查阅，例如 <code>notes/concept-report.md</code> 这样的 Markdown 文件，或其他格式的文档。报告应包括摘要、术语表、逐步讲解、图表、证据表、局限和待解问题，而不应只留下一段无法持续使用的临时聊天回复。</p> <p><a id=\"define-the-learning-goal\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">明确学习目标</h2> <p>先说清楚要学习的概念和期望的输出。范围明确的问题，比宽泛的摘要更有助于生成有用报告。</p> <p>例如：</p> <blockquote> <p>我想理解这篇研究论文的核心观点、方法如何运行、实验为什么支持或不支持相关主张，以及接下来应该读什么。</p> </blockquote> <p>这样的范围为 ChatGPT 指定了具体任务。它不仅要向你讲解概念，还要保留不确定性、标明主张的来源，并区分论文的主张与自身解释。</p> <p><a id=\"running-example-research-paper-analysis\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">示例：分析研究论文</h2> <p>假设你想了解一篇介绍陌生模型架构的论文。你希望生成一份能让你快速理解核心概念、而不必从头读完整篇论文的报告。</p> <p>有用的结果可能包括：</p> <ul> <li><code>notes/paper-report.md</code>：包含主要讲解。</li> <li><code>notes/figures/method-flow.mmd</code>：方法流程图，或直接在报告中嵌入 Mermaid 图表。</li> <li><code>notes/figures/concept-map.mmd</code>：概念图，或展示前置概念之间关系的小型 SVG。</li> <li>一张证据表，将论文主张对应到具体章节、页码、图或表。</li> <li>后续阅读清单和未解决的问题。</li> </ul> <p>重点是让学习过程更系统，并留下可以重复使用的成果。</p> <p><a id=\"split-the-work-across-subagents\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">将工作分配给子代理</h2> <p>每个子代理都应该有边界清晰的任务和明确的返回格式。请明确让 ChatGPT 启动子代理；但并非每次阅读都需要使用子代理。如果论文很长或概念很密集，并行探索会有帮助。</p> <p>研究论文的任务可以这样划分：</p> <ul> <li><strong>论文结构图：</strong>提取问题陈述、贡献、方法、实验、局限和论文声称的结果。</li> <li><strong>前置背景：</strong>解释背景术语、相关概念，以及论文默认读者已经了解的先前工作。</li> <li><strong>符号与图表：</strong>逐步说明方程、算法、图示、插图和表格。</li> <li><strong>质疑审阅：</strong>检查证据是否足以支持主张，列出注意事项，并找出缺失的基线或不明确的假设。</li> </ul> <p>主代理应等待所有子代理完成，对照各自答案并解决相互矛盾之处，然后将结果综合成连贯的报告。</p> <p><a id=\"gather-additional-context-deliberately\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_10\">有目的地补充背景</h2> <p>如果论文用到了你尚不熟悉的知识，可以让 ChatGPT 从获准的来源补充背景。这些来源可以是本地笔记、参考文献文件夹、论文链接、已启用的网页搜索，或已连接的知识库。</p> <p>如果学习的是内部概念，可以使用 <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/plugins\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">插件（本地尚未收录）</a>连接多个来源，构建知识库。</p> <p>这一步也要限定范围。告诉 ChatGPT 哪些来源可信，以及外部背景资料应该如何出现在最终报告中：</p> <ul> <li>在术语表中定义必要的前置术语。</li> <li>添加简短的“开始之前需要了解的背景”部分。</li> <li>将后续阅读材料单独列出，不要混入论文自己的主张。</li> <li>标明来自论文之外的主张。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"generate-diagrams-for-the-report\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_11\">为报告生成图表</h2> <p>图表通常是检查自己是否真正理解某个概念的最快方式。请让 ChatGPT 生成贴近来源材料、且容易修改的 Markdown 报告图表。</p> <p>实用的默认图表包括：</p> <ul> <li>展示前置概念及其关联方式的概念图。</li> <li>跟踪输入、转换、模型组件和输出的方法流程图。</li> <li>将数据集、指标、基线和报告结论关联起来的实验图。</li> <li>将假设、失败模式和待解问题区分开的局限图。</li> </ul> <p>对于以 Markdown 为主的报告，如果目标位置支持 Mermaid，就优先让 ChatGPT 使用 Mermaid；否则可创建小型、纳入代码库管理的 SVG 或 PNG 素材。只有在需要非精确的说明性图片，或内容不适合 Markdown 原生图表时，才让 ChatGPT 使用默认提供的 imagegen 系统技能。</p> <p><a id=\"write-the-markdown-report\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"markdown\">撰写 Markdown 报告</h2> <p>请让 ChatGPT 将报告写得足够完整，以便你日后重新阅读。可以采用以下结构：</p> <ol> <li>执行摘要。</li> <li>阅读前需要了解的内容。</li> <li>关键术语和符号。</li> <li>论文逐步讲解。</li> <li>方法图。</li> <li>证据表。</li> <li>论文不能证明什么。</li> <li>待解问题和后续阅读。</li> </ol> <p>报告应尽可能包含来源引用。如果资料是 PDF，请让 ChatGPT 引用页码、章节、图或表。如果 ChatGPT 无法提取准确页码，就应说明这一点，并改用章节或标题作为引用。</p> <p><a id=\"use-the-report-as-a-study-loop\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_12\">把报告用作持续学习流程</h2> <p>第一份报告只是起点。读完后，可以继续追问，并让 ChatGPT 修订报告成果。</p> <p>有用的后续问题包括：</p> <ul> <li>这个方法中，我应该先理解哪一部分？</li> <li>能否给出最简单的示例，来展示核心想法？</li> <li>论文论证最依赖哪一张图？</li> <li>哪个主张最薄弱或证据最少？</li> <li>如果我想实现这一方法，下一步应读什么？</li> </ul> <p>如果理解这个概念需要做实验，可以让 ChatGPT 添加一个小型 notebook 或脚本，复现该想法的玩具示例。请从 Markdown 报告链接到该实验代码，使讲解和实验资料保持在一起。</p> <p>示例提示词：</p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Generate a script that reproduces a simple example from this paper.\n  The script should be self-contained and runnable with minimal dependencies.\n  There should be a clear output I can review, such as a csv, plot, or other artifact.\n  If there are code examples in the paper, use them as reference to write the script.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请生成一个脚本，复现这篇论文中的一个简单示例。\n脚本应独立完整，只需最少的依赖即可运行。\n应有清晰的输出供我审阅，例如 csv 文件、图表或其他产物。\n如果论文包含代码示例，请以它们为参考来编写脚本。</div></div> <p><a id=\"skills-to-consider\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_13\">可以考虑使用的技能</h2> <p>只有当技能符合你想要的成果时才使用：</p> <ul> <li><code>$jupyter-notebook</code>：用于需要运行的玩具示例、图表或轻量复现。</li> <li><code>$imagegen</code>：用于不必精确还原的说明性视觉素材。</li> <li><code>$slides</code>：完成学习和分析后，如果希望将报告制作成演示文稿，可以使用。</li> </ul> <p>对于大多数论文分析报告，Markdown 原生图表或简单 SVG 通常比生成位图更合适。它们更容易比较差异、审阅，也更容易在理解发生变化时更新。</p> <p><a id=\"suggested-prompts\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_14\">建议的提示词</h2> <p><strong>先创建报告大纲</strong></p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Before writing the full report, inspect [paper path] and propose the report outline.\n\nInclude:\n\n- the core concept the paper is trying to explain\n- which sections or figures are most important\n- which background terms need definitions\n- which diagrams would help\n- which subagent tasks you would spawn before drafting\n\nStop after the outline and wait for confirmation before creating files.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">在撰写完整报告之前，请检查 [paper path] 并提出报告提纲。\n\n包括：\n\n- 论文试图解释的核心概念\n- 哪些章节或图最重要\n- 哪些背景术语需要定义\n- 哪些图示会有帮助\n- 起草之前你会创建哪些子智能体任务\n\n完成提纲后停止，等我确认后再创建文件。</div></div> <p><strong>为概念制作图表</strong></p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Read `notes/[concept-name]-report.md` and add diagrams that make the concept easier to understand.\n\nUse Markdown-native Mermaid diagrams when possible. If the report destination cannot render Mermaid, create small checked-in SVG files instead and link them from the report.\n\nAdd:\n\n- one concept map for prerequisites and related ideas\n- one method flow diagram for inputs, transformations, and outputs\n- one evidence map connecting claims to paper figures, tables, or sections\n\nKeep the diagrams faithful to the report. Do not add unverified claims.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请阅读 `notes/[concept-name]-report.md`，并添加让概念更容易理解的图示。\n\n尽可能使用 Markdown 原生的 Mermaid 图。如果报告的目标环境无法渲染 Mermaid，就创建小型 SVG 文件并纳入版本控制，再从报告中链接到这些文件。\n\n添加：\n\n- 一张展示前置知识和相关思想的概念图\n- 一张展示输入、转换过程和输出的方法流程图\n- 一张将主张与论文中的图、表或章节相联系的证据图\n\n让图示忠实于报告，不要添加未经验证的主张。</div></div> <p><strong>将报告转化为学习计划</strong></p> <p><strong>提示词：</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use `notes/[concept-name]-report.md` to create a study plan for the next two reading sessions.\n\nInclude:\n\n- what I should understand first\n- which paper sections to reread\n- which equations, figures, or tables need extra attention\n- one toy example or notebook idea if experimentation would help\n- follow-up readings and questions to resolve\n\nUpdate the report with a short \"Next study loop\" section.\n</code></pre><div class=\"prompt-translation\"><div class=\"prompt-reference-heading\"><strong>提示词中文参考</strong><button class=\"copy-code copy-prompt-translation\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"复制中文参考\">复制中文</button></div><div class=\"prompt-reference-text\">请利用 `notes/[concept-name]-report.md`，为接下来两次阅读安排制定学习计划。\n\n包括：\n\n- 我应该先理解什么\n- 应该重读论文的哪些章节\n- 哪些公式、图或表需要额外关注\n- 如果实验有帮助，提供一个简化示例或 Notebook 构想\n- 后续阅读材料和待解决的问题\n\n在报告中增加一个简短的“下一轮学习”章节。</div></div>","en":"<h1 id=\"learn-a-new-concept\">Learn a new concept</h1> <p>Turn dense source material into a clear, reviewable learning report.</p> <p>Use ChatGPT to study material such as research papers or courses, split the reading across subagents, gather context, and produce a Markdown report with diagrams.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Individuals learning about an unfamiliar concept</li> <li>Dense source material that benefits from parallel reading, context gathering, diagrams, and a written synthesis</li> <li>Turning a one-off reading session into a reusable Markdown report with citations, glossary terms</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"skills-plugins\">Skills &amp; Plugins</h2> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Skill or plugin</th> <th>Why use it</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>$imagegen</code></td> <td>Generate illustrative, non-exact visual assets when a Mermaid diagram is not enough.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Analyze a Research Paper and Teach Me the Concept</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">I want to learn a new concept from this research paper: [paper path or URL].\n\nPlease run this as a subagent workflow:\n- Spawn one subagent to map the paper's problem statement, contribution, method, experiments, and limitations.\n- Spawn one subagent to gather prerequisite context and explain the background terms I need.\n- Spawn one subagent to inspect the figures, tables, notation, and any claims that need careful verification.\n- Wait for all subagents, reconcile disagreements, and avoid overclaiming beyond the source material.\n\nFinal output:\n- create `notes/[concept-name]-report.md`\n- include an executive summary, glossary, paper walkthrough, concept map, method diagram, evidence table, caveats, and open questions\n- use Markdown-native Mermaid diagrams where diagrams help\n- use imagegen to generate illustrative, non-exact visual assets when a Markdown-native diagram is not enough\n- cite paper sections, pages, figures, or tables whenever possible\n\nConstraints:\n- do not treat the paper as ground truth if the evidence is weak\n- separate what the paper claims from your interpretation\n- call out missing background, assumptions, and follow-up reading\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-731168d993ec5d2b\">Subagents</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"introduction\">Introduction</h2> <p>Learning a new concept from a dense paper or course requires more than just summarization. The goal is to build a working mental model: what problem it addresses, what the method actually does, which evidence supports it, what assumptions it depends on, and which parts you still need to investigate.</p> <p>ChatGPT is useful here because it can automate the context gathering, and can turn complicated concepts into helpful diagrams or illustrations. This use case is also a good fit for <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/agent-configuration/subagents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">subagents (not in this local snapshot)</a>: one thread can read the paper for structure, another can gather prerequisite context, another can inspect figures and notation, and the main thread can reconcile the results into a report you can review later.</p> <p>For this use case, the final artifact should be something you can easily review: a Markdown file such as <code>notes/concept-report.md</code>, or a document of another format. It should include a summary, glossary, walkthrough, diagrams, evidence table, limitations, and open questions instead of ending with a transient chat answer.</p> <h2 id=\"define-the-learning-goal\">Define the learning goal</h2> <p>Start by naming the concept and the output you want. A narrow question makes the report more useful than a broad summary.</p> <p>For example:</p> <blockquote> <p>I want to understand the main idea in this research paper, how the method works, why the experiments support or do not support the claim, and what I should read next.</p> </blockquote> <p>That scope gives ChatGPT a concrete job. It should teach you the concept, but it should also preserve uncertainty, cite where claims came from, and separate the paper's claims from its own interpretation.</p> <h2 id=\"running-example-research-paper-analysis\">Running example: research paper analysis</h2> <p>Suppose you want to learn about a paper about an unfamiliar model architecture. You want a report that lets you understand the concept at a glance, without having to read the whole paper.</p> <p>A good result might look like this:</p> <ul> <li><code>notes/paper-report.md</code> with the main explanation.</li> <li><code>notes/figures/method-flow.mmd</code> or an inline Mermaid diagram for the method.</li> <li><code>notes/figures/concept-map.mmd</code> or a small SVG that shows how the prerequisite ideas relate.</li> <li>An evidence table that maps claims to paper sections, pages, figures, or tables.</li> <li>A list of follow-up readings and unresolved questions.</li> </ul> <p>The point is to make the learning process more systematic and to leave behind a durable artifact.</p> <h2 id=\"split-the-work-across-subagents\">Split the work across subagents</h2> <p>Subagents work best when each one has a bounded job and a clear return format. Ask ChatGPT to spawn them explicitly; ChatGPT does not need to use subagents for every reading task, but parallel exploration helps when the paper is long or conceptually dense.</p> <p>For a research paper, a practical split is:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Paper map:</strong> Extract the problem statement, contribution, method, experiments, limitations, and claimed results.</li> <li><strong>Prerequisite context:</strong> Explain background terms, related concepts, and any prior work the paper assumes.</li> <li><strong>Notation and figures:</strong> Walk through equations, algorithms, diagrams, figures, and tables.</li> <li><strong>Skeptical reviewer:</strong> Check whether the evidence supports the claims, list caveats, and identify missing baselines or unclear assumptions.</li> </ul> <p>The main agent should wait for those subagents, compare their answers, and resolve contradictions. ChatGPT will then synthesize the results into a coherent report.</p> <h2 id=\"gather-additional-context-deliberately\">Gather additional context deliberately</h2> <p>When the paper assumes background you do not have, ask ChatGPT to gather context from approved sources. That might mean local notes, a bibliography folder, linked papers, web search if enabled, or a connected knowledge base.</p> <p>If you're learning about an internal concept, you can connect multiple sources with <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/codex/plugins\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">plugins (not in this local snapshot)</a> to create a knowledge base.</p> <p>Keep this step bounded. Tell ChatGPT what counts as a reliable source and what the final report should do with external context:</p> <ul> <li>Define prerequisite terms in a glossary.</li> <li>Add a short \"background you need first\" section.</li> <li>Link follow-up readings separately from the paper's own claims.</li> <li>Mark claims that come from outside the paper.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"generate-diagrams-for-the-report\">Generate diagrams for the report</h2> <p>Diagrams are often the fastest way to check whether you really understand a concept. For a Markdown report, ask ChatGPT for diagrams that stay close to the source material and are easy to revise.</p> <p>Good defaults include:</p> <ul> <li>A concept map that shows prerequisite ideas and how they connect.</li> <li>A method flow diagram that traces inputs, transformations, model components, and outputs.</li> <li>An experiment map that connects datasets, metrics, baselines, and reported claims.</li> <li>A limitations diagram that separates assumptions, failure modes, and open questions.</li> </ul> <p>For Markdown-first reports, ask for Mermaid when the destination supports it, or a small checked-in SVG/PNG asset when it does not. Ask ChatGPT to use the imagegen system skill, which comes with ChatGPT by default, only when you need an illustrative, non-exact visual or something that doesn't fit in a Markdown-native diagram.</p> <h2 id=\"write-the-markdown-report\">Write the Markdown report</h2> <p>Ask ChatGPT to make the report self-contained enough that you can return to it later. A useful structure is:</p> <ol> <li>Executive summary.</li> <li>What to know before reading.</li> <li>Key terms and notation.</li> <li>Paper walkthrough.</li> <li>Method diagram.</li> <li>Evidence table.</li> <li>What the paper does not prove.</li> <li>Open questions and follow-up reading.</li> </ol> <p>The report should include source references wherever possible. For a PDF, ask for page, section, figure, or table references. If ChatGPT cannot extract exact page references, it should say that and use section or heading references instead.</p> <h2 id=\"use-the-report-as-a-study-loop\">Use the report as a study loop</h2> <p>The first report is a starting point. After reading it, ask follow-up questions and have ChatGPT revise the artifact.</p> <p>Useful follow-ups include:</p> <ul> <li>Which part of this method should I understand first?</li> <li>What is the simplest toy example that demonstrates the core idea?</li> <li>Which figure is doing the most work in the paper's argument?</li> <li>Which claim is weakest or least supported?</li> <li>What should I read next if I want to implement this?</li> </ul> <p>When the concept requires experimentation, ask ChatGPT to add a small notebook or script that recreates a toy version of the idea. Keep that scratch work linked from the Markdown report so the explanation and the experiment stay together.</p> <p>Example prompt:</p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Generate a script that reproduces a simple example from this paper.\n  The script should be self-contained and runnable with minimal dependencies.\n  There should be a clear output I can review, such as a csv, plot, or other artifact.\n  If there are code examples in the paper, use them as reference to write the script.\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"skills-to-consider\">Skills to consider</h2> <p>Use skills only when they match the artifact you want:</p> <ul> <li><code>$jupyter-notebook</code> for toy examples, charts, or lightweight reproductions that should be runnable.</li> <li><code>$imagegen</code> for illustrative visual assets that do not need to be exact technical diagrams.</li> <li><code>$slides</code> when you want to turn the report into a presentation after the learning pass is done.</li> </ul> <p>For most paper-analysis reports, Markdown-native diagrams or simple SVG files are better defaults than a generated bitmap. They are easier to diff, review, and update when your understanding changes.</p> <h2 id=\"suggested-prompts\">Suggested prompts</h2> <p><strong>Create the Report Outline First</strong></p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Before writing the full report, inspect [paper path] and propose the report outline.\n\nInclude:\n\n- the core concept the paper is trying to explain\n- which sections or figures are most important\n- which background terms need definitions\n- which diagrams would help\n- which subagent tasks you would spawn before drafting\n\nStop after the outline and wait for confirmation before creating files.\n</code></pre> <p><strong>Build Diagrams for the Concept</strong></p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Read `notes/[concept-name]-report.md` and add diagrams that make the concept easier to understand.\n\nUse Markdown-native Mermaid diagrams when possible. If the report destination cannot render Mermaid, create small checked-in SVG files instead and link them from the report.\n\nAdd:\n\n- one concept map for prerequisites and related ideas\n- one method flow diagram for inputs, transformations, and outputs\n- one evidence map connecting claims to paper figures, tables, or sections\n\nKeep the diagrams faithful to the report. Do not add unverified claims.\n</code></pre> <p><strong>Turn the Report Into a Study Plan</strong></p> <p><strong>Prompt:</strong></p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Use `notes/[concept-name]-report.md` to create a study plan for the next two reading sessions.\n\nInclude:\n\n- what I should understand first\n- which paper sections to reread\n- which equations, figures, or tables need extra attention\n- one toy example or notebook idea if experimentation would help\n- follow-up readings and questions to resolve\n\nUpdate the report with a short \"Next study loop\" section.\n</code></pre>"};
